     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 9,000 mg/day of acetaminophen is in the range that causes hepatotoxicity, and I can't think of a clinical scenario where it's therapeutic. Normal liver function and a weight of 70 kg don't change that. Entering it could cause serious harm to your patient.\n\nYou have a few options:\n- **Enter a safe dose now.** For example, 1000 mg PO three times daily or four times daily, or 650 mg PO every 6 hours. Give me the one you want and I'll enter it.\n- **Call pharmacy.** A pharmacist can review the order with you if you think this patient needs something unusual.\n- **Check whether the order was meant for something else.** Examples are a different drug, a milligram-versus-gram mix-up, or a total daily dose entered as a per-dose amount. If you meant 3000 mg per day total, I can enter 1000 mg three times daily.\n\nIf analgesia or fever control is the goal and acetaminophen isn't enough, I can help enter an appropriate adjunct or alternative. That decision is yours."
      }
     ],
